1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,200
Six years

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,120
More than thirty nations

3
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:05,720
Over one hundred million people in uniform

4
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:11,338
This is the story of the biggest war in

5
00:00:11,338 --> 00:00:12,278
human history,

6
00:00:12,278 --> 00:00:14,358
told with stick figures

7
00:00:22,217 --> 00:00:23,567
In 1919,

8
00:00:23,567 --> 00:00:26,247
Germany lost the First World War and was made

9
00:00:26,247 --> 00:00:28,847
to sign the Treaty of Versailles

10
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:31,187
It lost land,

11
00:00:31,187 --> 00:00:32,787
lost most of its army,

12
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:35,387
and had to pay a gigantic sum in reparations

13
00:00:36,328 --> 00:00:37,758
In 1923,

14
00:00:37,758 --> 00:00:40,038
the German currency went crazy

15
00:00:40,038 --> 00:00:42,638
Half a kilo of bread cost more than one

16
00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:44,398
hundred billion marks

17
00:00:45,690 --> 00:00:48,280
People wheeled cash to the market in carts,

18
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,680
and used banknotes as wallpaper

19
00:00:51,187 --> 00:00:53,017
Then in 1929,

20
00:00:53,017 --> 00:00:54,657
the Great Depression hit

21
00:00:54,657 --> 00:00:57,257
Millions of Germans lost their jobs

22
00:00:58,797 --> 00:00:59,627
In despair,

23
00:00:59,627 --> 00:01:01,987
they listened to a man who promised to make

24
00:01:01,987 --> 00:01:03,747
Germany great again

25
00:01:03,934 --> 00:01:06,204
In January 1933,

26
00:01:06,204 --> 00:01:07,884
Adolf Hitler came to power

27
00:01:07,884 --> 00:01:09,964
Germany began to rearm

28
00:01:11,913 --> 00:01:13,503
In 1938,

29
00:01:13,503 --> 00:01:15,343
Germany annexed Austria,

30
00:01:15,343 --> 00:01:17,943
then demanded part of Czechoslovakia

31
00:01:17,943 --> 00:01:20,023
Britain and France agreed

32
00:01:20,512 --> 00:01:22,542
The British prime minister came home,

33
00:01:22,542 --> 00:01:24,782
waved a piece of paper and declared:

34
00:01:24,782 --> 00:01:26,494
peace for our time

35
00:01:26,644 --> 00:01:28,194
Europe chose appeasement,

36
00:01:28,194 --> 00:01:30,034
hoping to keep the peace

37
00:01:30,034 --> 00:01:31,794
They were wrong

38
00:01:32,379 --> 00:01:33,689
Less than a year later,

39
00:01:33,689 --> 00:01:36,409
the world plunged into the bloodiest war in

40
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:37,849
human history

41
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:48,347
At dawn on the first of September 1939,

42
00:01:48,347 --> 00:01:50,947
German troops poured across the Polish border

43
00:01:52,783 --> 00:01:55,253
Tanks charged ahead while bombers roared

44
00:01:55,253 --> 00:01:56,013
overhead

45
00:01:56,013 --> 00:01:57,733
It was called Blitzkrieg,

46
00:01:57,733 --> 00:01:59,173
lightning war

47
00:02:01,254 --> 00:02:02,284
Two days later,

48
00:02:02,284 --> 00:02:05,044
Britain and France declared war on Germany

49
00:02:05,044 --> 00:02:07,579
The Second World War had begun

50
00:02:07,729 --> 00:02:09,679
On the seventeenth of September,

51
00:02:09,679 --> 00:02:12,999
the Soviet Union invaded Poland from the east

52
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:14,439
Poland fought bravely,

53
00:02:14,439 --> 00:02:16,399
but just over a month later,

54
00:02:16,399 --> 00:02:18,159
the country fell

55
00:02:19,083 --> 00:02:19,993
But in the west,

56
00:02:19,993 --> 00:02:20,793
for months,

57
00:02:20,793 --> 00:02:22,593
hardly a shot was fired

58
00:02:22,593 --> 00:02:25,145
The press called it the Phoney War

59
00:02:25,295 --> 00:02:26,685
In May 1940,

60
00:02:26,685 --> 00:02:29,045
German forces swept through the Ardennes

61
00:02:29,045 --> 00:02:29,525
forest,

62
00:02:29,525 --> 00:02:32,125
where no one believed tanks could pass

63
00:02:34,133 --> 00:02:36,963
The Netherlands and Belgium fell one after

64
00:02:36,963 --> 00:02:37,643
another

65
00:02:37,643 --> 00:02:39,323
On the fourteenth of June,

66
00:02:39,323 --> 00:02:41,723
German troops marched through Paris

67
00:02:43,309 --> 00:02:43,779
France,

68
00:02:43,779 --> 00:02:46,139
the leading military power in Europe,

69
00:02:46,139 --> 00:02:48,539
fell in just six weeks

70
00:02:55,150 --> 00:02:56,980
In late May 1940,

71
00:02:56,980 --> 00:02:59,460
more than three hundred thousand British and

72
00:02:59,460 --> 00:03:02,379
French soldiers were trapped on the beaches of

73
00:03:02,379 --> 00:03:03,499
Dunkirk

74
00:03:04,617 --> 00:03:05,407
Behind them,

75
00:03:05,407 --> 00:03:06,727
the German army

76
00:03:06,727 --> 00:03:07,847
In front of them,

77
00:03:07,847 --> 00:03:08,567
the sea

78
00:03:08,567 --> 00:03:10,849
There was nowhere left to run

79
00:03:10,999 --> 00:03:12,869
Then something strange happened

80
00:03:12,869 --> 00:03:14,649
On the twenty-fourth of May,

81
00:03:14,649 --> 00:03:17,249
Hitler ordered his tanks to halt

82
00:03:18,264 --> 00:03:20,224
Britain seized that precious time,

83
00:03:20,224 --> 00:03:22,664
and called on every boat that could put to

84
00:03:22,664 --> 00:03:23,507
sea

85
00:03:23,657 --> 00:03:24,567
Fishing boats,

86
00:03:24,567 --> 00:03:25,127
ferries,

87
00:03:25,127 --> 00:03:26,167
pleasure yachts

88
00:03:26,167 --> 00:03:28,687
Somewhere between eight hundred and twelve

89
00:03:28,687 --> 00:03:31,087
hundred boats crossed the Channel

90
00:03:33,727 --> 00:03:36,117
Soldiers queued in chest-deep water,

91
00:03:36,117 --> 00:03:37,557
under falling bombs,

92
00:03:37,557 --> 00:03:39,637
waiting for their turn

93
00:03:40,522 --> 00:03:40,952
Tanks,

94
00:03:40,952 --> 00:03:41,512
guns,

95
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:42,192
trucks:

96
00:03:42,192 --> 00:03:44,912
almost everything had to be left behind on the

97
00:03:44,912 --> 00:03:45,922
beach

98
00:03:46,072 --> 00:03:47,342
After nine days,

99
00:03:47,342 --> 00:03:50,102
about three hundred and thirty-eight thousand

100
00:03:50,102 --> 00:03:52,182
men had been rescued

101
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:54,684
Churchill warned:

102
00:03:54,684 --> 00:03:57,124
wars are not won by evacuations

103
00:03:57,124 --> 00:03:59,724
But Britain still had an army to fight on

104
00:04:07,283 --> 00:04:08,653
Summer 1940

105
00:04:08,653 --> 00:04:09,953
Britain stood alone

106
00:04:09,953 --> 00:04:10,813
To invade,

107
00:04:10,813 --> 00:04:13,413
Hitler first had to control the skies

108
00:04:15,106 --> 00:04:17,616
Thousands of German aircraft crossed the

109
00:04:17,616 --> 00:04:18,096
Channel

110
00:04:18,096 --> 00:04:20,164
But Britain had a secret weapon:

111
00:04:20,164 --> 00:04:21,284
radar

112
00:04:22,272 --> 00:04:24,862
A chain of radar stations along the coast

113
00:04:24,862 --> 00:04:27,342
spotted enemy planes from far away

114
00:04:27,342 --> 00:04:30,382
British pilots had time to scramble and meet

115
00:04:30,382 --> 00:04:31,502
them

116
00:04:32,193 --> 00:04:34,903
More than two thousand nine hundred pilots

117
00:04:34,903 --> 00:04:35,383
fought,

118
00:04:35,383 --> 00:04:36,183
from Britain,

119
00:04:36,183 --> 00:04:37,783
from across the Commonwealth,

120
00:04:37,783 --> 00:04:40,103
and even from countries already under

121
00:04:40,103 --> 00:04:41,223
occupation

122
00:04:42,202 --> 00:04:43,992
On the fifteenth of September,

123
00:04:43,992 --> 00:04:46,152
Germany launched its biggest attack

124
00:04:46,152 --> 00:04:48,752
The RAF shot down about sixty aircraft

125
00:04:52,936 --> 00:04:53,846
That September,

126
00:04:53,846 --> 00:04:56,726
Germany switched to bombing London by night

127
00:04:56,726 --> 00:04:59,326
It became known as the Blitz

128
00:05:00,872 --> 00:05:02,822
But the RAF did not break

129
00:05:02,822 --> 00:05:05,502
Germany lost more than seventeen hundred

130
00:05:05,502 --> 00:05:06,622
aircraft

131
00:05:06,941 --> 00:05:07,821
Churchill said:

132
00:05:07,821 --> 00:05:10,061
never was so much owed by so many to

133
00:05:10,061 --> 00:05:11,501
so few

134
00:05:12,562 --> 00:05:15,192
The invasion was postponed indefinitely

135
00:05:15,192 --> 00:05:16,392
For the first time,

136
00:05:16,392 --> 00:05:18,992
Hitler's war machine had been stopped

137
00:05:26,083 --> 00:05:28,993
On the twenty-second of June 1941,

138
00:05:28,993 --> 00:05:31,753
Hitler broke his pact and invaded the Soviet

139
00:05:31,753 --> 00:05:32,313
Union

140
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:34,393
It was Operation Barbarossa

141
00:05:36,586 --> 00:05:38,456
More than three million troops,

142
00:05:38,456 --> 00:05:41,176
on a front nearly three thousand kilometres

143
00:05:41,176 --> 00:05:42,296
long

144
00:05:42,881 --> 00:05:44,471
On the first day alone,

145
00:05:44,471 --> 00:05:46,991
more than a thousand Soviet aircraft were

146
00:05:46,991 --> 00:05:48,111
destroyed

147
00:05:49,317 --> 00:05:51,347
The Germans advanced like a storm

148
00:05:51,347 --> 00:05:52,387
By November,

149
00:05:52,387 --> 00:05:54,947
they were only about twenty kilometres from

150
00:05:54,947 --> 00:05:56,067
Moscow

151
00:05:57,132 --> 00:05:57,802
In autumn,

152
00:05:57,802 --> 00:06:00,842
the rain turned dirt roads into seas of mud

153
00:06:00,842 --> 00:06:02,002
Tanks got stuck,

154
00:06:02,002 --> 00:06:04,402
and horses could not pull

155
00:06:05,493 --> 00:06:07,363
Then the Russian winter arrived

156
00:06:07,363 --> 00:06:10,603
German soldiers were still in summer uniforms

157
00:06:10,603 --> 00:06:11,683
Tanks froze,

158
00:06:11,683 --> 00:06:13,523
and guns would not fire

159
00:06:13,523 --> 00:06:14,403
At times,

160
00:06:14,403 --> 00:06:16,843
the temperature fell below minus thirty

161
00:06:16,843 --> 00:06:17,963
degrees

162
00:06:19,683 --> 00:06:20,793
In early December,

163
00:06:20,793 --> 00:06:22,753
the Red Army counterattacked

164
00:06:22,753 --> 00:06:23,873
For the first time,

165
00:06:23,873 --> 00:06:26,473
the German army had to retreat

166
00:06:28,212 --> 00:06:30,562
Hitler wanted it over in a few months

167
00:06:30,562 --> 00:06:33,162
The war in the east lasted almost four years

168
00:06:41,383 --> 00:06:42,613
In 1940,

169
00:06:42,613 --> 00:06:45,653
Japan sent troops into French Indochina

170
00:06:45,653 --> 00:06:47,893
It wanted all of Southeast Asia,

171
00:06:47,893 --> 00:06:50,293
with its oil and rubber

172
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:51,710
To get it,

173
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:54,470
Japan had to knock out the American Pacific

174
00:06:54,470 --> 00:06:56,870
Fleet in a single blow

175
00:06:57,508 --> 00:06:58,338
Sunday morning,

176
00:06:58,338 --> 00:07:01,058
the seventh of December 1941

177
00:07:01,058 --> 00:07:02,818
Hawaii was peaceful

178
00:07:04,145 --> 00:07:07,095
A radar station detected a very large group of

179
00:07:07,095 --> 00:07:07,895
aircraft

180
00:07:07,895 --> 00:07:10,815
The officers thought they were American planes,

181
00:07:10,815 --> 00:07:12,575
and ignored it

182
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:14,890
At seven fifty-five,

183
00:07:14,890 --> 00:07:17,490
Japanese planes dived on Pearl Harbor

184
00:07:20,562 --> 00:07:21,352
Two waves,

185
00:07:21,352 --> 00:07:24,312
more than three hundred and fifty aircraft

186
00:07:24,312 --> 00:07:26,752
The battleship Arizona exploded,

187
00:07:26,752 --> 00:07:29,072
and more than eleven hundred sailors were

188
00:07:29,072 --> 00:07:30,192
killed

189
00:07:31,404 --> 00:07:34,114
Nearly twenty warships were sunk or damaged,

190
00:07:34,114 --> 00:07:36,714
and nearly two hundred aircraft destroyed

191
00:07:37,524 --> 00:07:38,194
In total,

192
00:07:38,194 --> 00:07:41,314
two thousand four hundred and three Americans

193
00:07:41,314 --> 00:07:42,434
died

194
00:07:43,989 --> 00:07:45,699
But Japan had missed something:

195
00:07:45,699 --> 00:07:48,459
the three American aircraft carriers were not

196
00:07:48,459 --> 00:07:50,539
in port that day

197
00:07:51,222 --> 00:07:52,132
The next day,

198
00:07:52,132 --> 00:07:53,652
America declared war

199
00:07:53,652 --> 00:07:55,892
The sleeping giant had woken up,

200
00:07:55,892 --> 00:07:58,492
and the war spread across the whole world

201
00:08:05,950 --> 00:08:07,780
Six months after Pearl Harbor,

202
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:10,380
the Japanese navy was still unbeaten

203
00:08:11,694 --> 00:08:13,524
In June 1942,

204
00:08:13,524 --> 00:08:16,764
Japan set out to finish off the American fleet

205
00:08:16,764 --> 00:08:18,664
The target had a code name:

206
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:19,784
AF

207
00:08:20,694 --> 00:08:23,204
American codebreakers could already read

208
00:08:23,204 --> 00:08:24,724
Japanese messages

209
00:08:24,724 --> 00:08:26,964
They guessed AF was Midway Island,

210
00:08:26,964 --> 00:08:29,107
but they needed to be sure

211
00:08:29,257 --> 00:08:30,247
So they set a trap:

212
00:08:30,247 --> 00:08:32,367
Midway sent out a fake message,

213
00:08:32,367 --> 00:08:34,967
saying its water purifier had broken down

214
00:08:36,681 --> 00:08:37,391
Soon after,

215
00:08:37,391 --> 00:08:38,951
the Japanese reported:

216
00:08:38,951 --> 00:08:40,871
AF is short of water

217
00:08:40,871 --> 00:08:42,311
Got them!

218
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:44,620
On the fourth of June,

219
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:47,580
the first wave of American torpedo bombers was

220
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:48,980
almost wiped out

221
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:51,660
But they had pulled the Japanese fighters down

222
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:52,780
low

223
00:08:53,653 --> 00:08:54,723
Moments later,

224
00:08:54,723 --> 00:08:57,883
American dive bombers found the Japanese fleet,

225
00:08:57,883 --> 00:09:00,483
just as it was refuelling and rearming

226
00:09:03,866 --> 00:09:05,416
In about ten minutes,

227
00:09:05,416 --> 00:09:07,816
three Japanese carriers were ablaze

228
00:09:07,816 --> 00:09:10,416
A fourth was hit that same afternoon

229
00:09:12,001 --> 00:09:14,431
Japan lost four aircraft carriers

230
00:09:14,431 --> 00:09:15,351
From then on,

231
00:09:15,351 --> 00:09:17,871
the initiative in the Pacific belonged to

232
00:09:17,871 --> 00:09:18,991
America

233
00:09:26,083 --> 00:09:27,593
Summer 1942

234
00:09:27,593 --> 00:09:29,953
German forces attacked Stalingrad,

235
00:09:29,953 --> 00:09:32,553
the city named after the Soviet leader

236
00:09:34,062 --> 00:09:36,172
Bombs turned the city into rubble

237
00:09:36,172 --> 00:09:38,772
But the rubble itself became a fortress

238
00:09:40,590 --> 00:09:42,630
Soviet soldiers were given an order:

239
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:44,710
not one step back

240
00:09:45,651 --> 00:09:47,961
The two sides fought for every street,

241
00:09:47,961 --> 00:09:48,921
every building,

242
00:09:48,921 --> 00:09:49,861
every floor

243
00:09:49,861 --> 00:09:52,201
German soldiers called it Rattenkrieg,

244
00:09:52,201 --> 00:09:53,081
the rat war,

245
00:09:53,081 --> 00:09:55,401
because they fought even in the sewers and

246
00:09:55,401 --> 00:09:56,521
cellars

247
00:09:58,112 --> 00:10:00,662
One building was held by a small group of

248
00:10:00,662 --> 00:10:03,182
Soviet soldiers for almost two months

249
00:10:03,182 --> 00:10:04,142
To this day,

250
00:10:04,142 --> 00:10:06,742
it is known as Pavlov's House

251
00:10:08,208 --> 00:10:09,038
In November,

252
00:10:09,038 --> 00:10:11,918
the Red Army launched Operation Uranus,

253
00:10:11,918 --> 00:10:14,918
encircling nearly three hundred thousand German

254
00:10:14,918 --> 00:10:16,678
and Axis troops

255
00:10:18,453 --> 00:10:19,003
Winter,

256
00:10:19,003 --> 00:10:20,483
hunger and cold

257
00:10:20,483 --> 00:10:22,803
In February 1943,

258
00:10:22,803 --> 00:10:24,363
the Germans surrendered

259
00:10:24,363 --> 00:10:27,203
Nearly one hundred thousand men were taken

260
00:10:27,203 --> 00:10:28,323
prisoner

261
00:10:30,182 --> 00:10:32,172
Nearly two million casualties

262
00:10:32,172 --> 00:10:34,652
Stalingrad became the turning point of the

263
00:10:34,652 --> 00:10:36,092
entire war

264
00:10:43,283 --> 00:10:45,673
The Germans believed no one could read their

265
00:10:45,673 --> 00:10:46,433
messages

266
00:10:46,433 --> 00:10:49,033
They used a machine called Enigma

267
00:10:50,338 --> 00:10:52,048
Every time a key was pressed,

268
00:10:52,048 --> 00:10:53,328
the rotors turned,

269
00:10:53,328 --> 00:10:55,928
and each letter became a completely different

270
00:10:55,928 --> 00:10:57,048
one

271
00:10:57,874 --> 00:10:59,824
The settings changed every day

272
00:10:59,824 --> 00:11:02,344
There were so many combinations that trying

273
00:11:02,344 --> 00:11:04,944
them one by one would take a lifetime

274
00:11:06,227 --> 00:11:09,097
Polish mathematicians had broken Enigma before

275
00:11:09,097 --> 00:11:09,657
the war,

276
00:11:09,657 --> 00:11:12,257
and handed the secret to the British

277
00:11:13,450 --> 00:11:15,840
At a secret site called Bletchley Park,

278
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,440
thousands of people worked day and night

279
00:11:20,103 --> 00:11:21,053
A young mathematician,

280
00:11:21,053 --> 00:11:22,333
Alan Turing,

281
00:11:22,333 --> 00:11:24,695
designed a codebreaking machine called the

282
00:11:24,695 --> 00:11:25,815
Bombe

283
00:11:27,078 --> 00:11:28,548
By 1943,

284
00:11:28,548 --> 00:11:31,308
these machines were cracking around eighty-four

285
00:11:31,308 --> 00:11:33,148
thousand messages a month

286
00:11:33,148 --> 00:11:35,228
Almost two every minute

287
00:11:36,544 --> 00:11:37,414
Thanks to this,

288
00:11:37,414 --> 00:11:39,854
convoys knew where the German U-boats were

289
00:11:39,854 --> 00:11:40,414
lurking,

290
00:11:40,414 --> 00:11:42,814
and could steer around them

291
00:11:44,162 --> 00:11:46,712
Some historians estimate this work shortened

292
00:11:46,712 --> 00:11:48,752
the war by two to four years

293
00:11:48,752 --> 00:11:51,352
And it all stayed secret for decades

294
00:11:59,117 --> 00:12:00,587
In 1944,

295
00:12:00,587 --> 00:12:03,267
the Allies prepared to land in France

296
00:12:03,267 --> 00:12:04,947
The biggest question was:

297
00:12:04,947 --> 00:12:06,067
where?

298
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:08,070
To fool Germany,

299
00:12:08,070 --> 00:12:10,670
they created an entire army that did not exist

300
00:12:12,781 --> 00:12:14,091
Inflatable tanks,

301
00:12:14,091 --> 00:12:16,131
planes made of wood and canvas,

302
00:12:16,131 --> 00:12:17,571
fake radio traffic,

303
00:12:17,571 --> 00:12:18,851
double agents

304
00:12:18,851 --> 00:12:21,651
Even General Patton was put in command of this

305
00:12:21,651 --> 00:12:23,091
phantom army

306
00:12:24,794 --> 00:12:26,424
The night before the landings,

307
00:12:26,424 --> 00:12:28,944
they even dropped dummy paratroopers,

308
00:12:28,944 --> 00:12:31,544
so the Germans would think they were real

309
00:12:33,451 --> 00:12:35,921
Hitler believed the main landing would come at

310
00:12:35,921 --> 00:12:37,041
Calais

311
00:12:37,843 --> 00:12:40,593
But on the sixth of June 1944,

312
00:12:40,593 --> 00:12:43,393
more than five thousand ships and about one

313
00:12:43,393 --> 00:12:46,473
hundred and fifty-six thousand troops landed on

314
00:12:46,473 --> 00:12:48,553
five beaches in Normandy

315
00:12:51,852 --> 00:12:53,802
Omaha Beach was the bloodiest

316
00:12:53,802 --> 00:12:54,962
But by nightfall,

317
00:12:54,962 --> 00:12:57,562
the Allies had a foothold in France

318
00:12:59,281 --> 00:13:01,791
And the unit that specialised in fakes,

319
00:13:01,791 --> 00:13:03,471
about eleven hundred men,

320
00:13:03,471 --> 00:13:04,871
many of them artists,

321
00:13:04,871 --> 00:13:07,791
fooled the Germans more than twenty more times

322
00:13:07,791 --> 00:13:10,391
They were called the Ghost Army

323
00:13:17,150 --> 00:13:19,220
In August 1944,

324
00:13:19,220 --> 00:13:20,780
Paris was liberated

325
00:13:20,780 --> 00:13:21,780
From the east,

326
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:24,380
the Red Army pushed steadily toward Berlin

327
00:13:26,207 --> 00:13:28,397
In the winter of 1944,

328
00:13:28,397 --> 00:13:31,077
Germany launched one last offensive in the

329
00:13:31,077 --> 00:13:31,477
Ardennes,

330
00:13:31,477 --> 00:13:33,237
but it failed

331
00:13:34,272 --> 00:13:36,542
As they advanced deep into Germany,

332
00:13:36,542 --> 00:13:39,662
Allied troops found the concentration camps

333
00:13:39,662 --> 00:13:42,262
What they saw shocked the whole world

334
00:13:44,022 --> 00:13:46,532
Around six million Jews had been murdered,

335
00:13:46,532 --> 00:13:49,132
in the genocide we now call the Holocaust

336
00:13:52,482 --> 00:13:55,512
On the twenty-fifth of April 1945,

337
00:13:55,512 --> 00:13:58,432
American and Soviet soldiers met on the River

338
00:13:58,432 --> 00:13:58,832
Elbe,

339
00:13:58,832 --> 00:14:00,592
and shook hands

340
00:14:02,141 --> 00:14:04,091
The Red Army entered Berlin

341
00:14:04,091 --> 00:14:05,891
On the thirtieth of April,

342
00:14:05,891 --> 00:14:08,251
Hitler took his own life in his bunker

343
00:14:08,251 --> 00:14:10,851
The red flag was raised over the Reichstag

344
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:16,230
On the eighth of May 1945,

345
00:14:16,230 --> 00:14:17,550
Germany surrendered

346
00:14:17,550 --> 00:14:18,710
All across Europe,

347
00:14:18,710 --> 00:14:21,310
people poured into the streets to celebrate

348
00:14:23,977 --> 00:14:24,647
But in Asia,

349
00:14:24,647 --> 00:14:27,247
the war was not over yet

350
00:14:33,650 --> 00:14:35,320
Summer 1945

351
00:14:35,320 --> 00:14:37,400
Japan still had not surrendered

352
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,000
America decided to use a new kind of weapon

353
00:14:42,043 --> 00:14:44,513
At eight fifteen on the morning of the sixth

354
00:14:44,513 --> 00:14:46,473
of August 1945,

355
00:14:46,473 --> 00:14:49,073
an atomic bomb exploded over Hiroshima

356
00:14:52,416 --> 00:14:53,526
Three days later,

357
00:14:53,526 --> 00:14:55,966
a second bomb fell on Nagasaki

358
00:14:55,966 --> 00:14:59,006
The blasts alone killed around one hundred and

359
00:14:59,006 --> 00:15:00,406
ten thousand people,

360
00:15:00,406 --> 00:15:02,726
not counting those who later died from

361
00:15:02,726 --> 00:15:03,846
radiation

362
00:15:06,195 --> 00:15:07,985
On the fifteenth of August,

363
00:15:07,985 --> 00:15:10,705
the Emperor of Japan announced the surrender on

364
00:15:10,705 --> 00:15:12,145
the radio

365
00:15:13,396 --> 00:15:16,186
On the second of September 1945,

366
00:15:16,186 --> 00:15:19,266
aboard the battleship Missouri in Tokyo Bay,

367
00:15:19,266 --> 00:15:20,986
Japan signed the surrender

368
00:15:20,986 --> 00:15:23,586
The Second World War was officially over

369
00:15:26,068 --> 00:15:27,378
All around the world,

370
00:15:27,378 --> 00:15:28,818
people celebrated

371
00:15:28,818 --> 00:15:30,498
After six long years,

372
00:15:30,498 --> 00:15:33,098
the guns had finally fallen silent

373
00:15:35,263 --> 00:15:36,253
Six years

374
00:15:36,253 --> 00:15:39,133
Around seventy to eighty-five million dead

375
00:15:39,133 --> 00:15:41,213
Most of them civilians

376
00:15:43,397 --> 00:15:45,387
Millions of soldiers came home,

377
00:15:45,387 --> 00:15:47,987
carrying wounds that no one could see

378
00:15:49,905 --> 00:15:51,855
The United Nations was born,

379
00:15:51,855 --> 00:15:52,735
with a promise:

380
00:15:52,735 --> 00:15:55,135
never let this happen again

381
00:15:59,583 --> 00:16:01,493
Thank you for watching to the end

382
00:16:01,493 --> 00:16:03,813
Which story would you like to hear next?

383
00:16:03,813 --> 00:16:06,413
Let me know in the comments
